  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on self-reported perceived flexibility and diary data from a single subdistrict in Beijing, which may limit generalizability to other urban contexts.",
      "what": "This study analyzes how foreground and background information and communications technology (ICT) use relates to perceived spatial and temporal flexibility. Using a 2-day combined daily activity and internet activity diary survey from 2021 in Qinghe, Beijing, the researchers applied multilevel ordered logistic regression at the activity level and ordinary least squares regression at the daily level to compare light and heavy ICT users.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that the impact of digitalization on daily schedules depends on how technology is used; while foreground ICT use generally correlates with higher perceived flexibility, frequent background ICT use among heavy users is actually associated with lower perceived temporal flexibility."
    }
